Experiment 3: Create and Merge Branches in Git

Aim

To create a new branch, make changes in the branch, and merge it into the main branch using Git commands.

Requirements

- Git installed on the computer
- GitHub account
- A local Git repository

Procedure

1. Open the project repository.
2. Check the current branch.
3. Create a new branch.
4. Switch to the new branch.
5. Create a file and commit the changes.
6. Switch back to the main branch.
7. Merge the new branch into the main branch.
8. Verify the merged changes.

Commands

git clone https://github.com/Sudiksha784/My-First-Git-Project.git
cd My-First-Git-Project
git branch
git switch -c feature
echo "Branch experiment" > feature.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Add feature file"
git switch main
git merge feature
git push origin main

Explanation of Commands

- "git clone URL" – Downloads the repository from GitHub.
- "cd My-First-Git-Project" – Opens the project folder.
- "git branch" – Displays the available local branches.
- "git switch -c feature" – Creates and switches to a new branch named "feature".
- "echo "Branch experiment" > feature.txt" – Creates a file.
- "git add ." – Stages the changes.
- "git commit -m "Add feature file"" – Saves the changes.
- "git switch main" – Switches to the main branch.
- "git merge feature" – Merges the feature branch into main.
- "git push origin main" – Uploads the merged changes to GitHub.

Expected Output

The feature branch is created, and its changes are merged into the main branch successfully.

Result

Thus, a new Git branch was created, modified, and merged into the main branch successfully.

Important Note

If the repository is already cloned, do not clone it again. Open the existing folder and continue from "git switch -c feature". If the branch already exists, switch to it or choose another branch name. After merging, the feature file should appear in the main branch.